## TTA-UC from fast and long-range triplet exciton diffusion
The \(I_{\mathrm{th}}\) values of MOFs were further scrutinized by quantifying the triplet exciton diffusion constants, \(D_{\mathrm{T}}\) , in the crystalline frameworks. The threshold is expressed as a function of the system's fundamental parameters using35
\[
I_{\mathrm{th}}=(\alpha\varPhi_{\mathrm{FT}}\gamma_{\mathrm{TT}})^{-1}(\tau_{\mathrm{T}})^{-2}
\]
where \(\alpha\) is the absorption coefficient at the excitation wavelength in \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , \(\tau_{\mathrm{T}}\) is the lifetime of the acceptor triplet, and \(\gamma_{\mathrm{TT}}\) is the second-order annihilation constant for TTA. The \(\tau_{\mathrm{T}}\) values were measured from the decay time of the upconverted emission by considering the relationship \(I_{\mathrm{UC}}(t)\propto\exp(-t/\tau_{\mathrm{UC}})\,{=}\,\exp(-2t/\tau_{\mathrm{T}})\) in the longer timescale region, when the annihilation efficiency
\[
D_{\mathrm{T}}=(\gamma_{\mathrm{TT}})(8\pi a_0)^{-1}
\]
organization, and overlap of the \(\pi\) orbitals to achieve an efficient triplet exciton diffusion. Note that significantly long, micrometre-scale diffusion lengths were observed for the MOF crystals. This was realized thanks to the combination of the long triplet exciton lifetime \(\tau_{\mathrm{T}}\) in the crystalline framework and the fast triplet migration within the chromophores, whose spatial alignment was controlled properly. The observed triplet diffusion constant is consistent with previous examples. For example, the \(D_{\mathrm{T}}\) values in MOFs are comparable to pure anthracene \((1.5\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) ; refs 41,42). Care should be taken in strictly comparing the measured diffusivities with these previous reports, because they were obtained by different experimental methods, and the diffusion constant and length may depend slightly on the techniques employed43.
The TTA-UC quantum yield, \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{UC}}\) , was then determined using the DPA–PtOEP mixed solution as a standard. Note that the TTA-UC process uses two photons to produce one photon, and therefore the theoretical maximum of \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{UC}}\) is considered as \(50\%\) (refs 7,12). The \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{UC}}\) values obtained for MOFs 1, 2 and 3 were \(0.43\%\) , \(0.34\%\) and \(4.3\%\) , respectively, in the high-excitation regime where the conversion yield is constant (slope \(=1\) for the data in Fig. 3d). To extract the intrinsic parameters of the MOFs, these \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{UC}}\) values were normalized to donor-to-acceptor energy transfer efficiency, \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{ET}}\) . The normalized values of \(3.6\%\) , \(4.3\%\) and \(7.3\%\) obtained for MOFs 1, 2 and 3 are comparable to other efficient TTA-UC systems12. This is the first attempt to affirm the potential of MOFs as lightharvesting platforms for TTA-UC, and we presume that the UC efficiency can be improved further by employing recently developed strongly emissive MOFs with \(\varPhi_{\mathrm{ET}}\) values close to unity44.
