## Outlook
By exploiting MOF crystalline systems, we successfully maximized UC efficiency under very weak excitation intensities, comparable to or less than sunlight. This breakthrough was based on combining successful donor–acceptor integration into a solid upconverter and precise control of the acceptor spatial organization in crystalline MOFs, which led to remarkably fast and long-range triplet exciton diffusion. Our results indicate that precise control of the overlap between the aromatic \( \pi \) planes is of paramount importance in rationally designing triplet energy-harvesting materials with a high triplet exciton diffusivity. Based on the effective triplet diffusion in the MOFs, we achieved a quantitative TTA process in the solid state even under excitation intensities much lower than solar irradiance. It paves the way for sensitized UC materials that can be applied in solar energy technologies.
A number of promising directions for future work can be pursued to achieve the theoretical maximum photon upconversion yield of \( 50\% \) under solar irradiance. The donor-to-acceptor TTET efficiency, \( \Phi_{\mathrm{ET}} \), can be improved by constructing MOFs with larger pore sizes and accommodating the donor molecules in the nanospaces. Together with this technique, higher UC quantum yields can be obtained by employing MOFs with enhanced fluorescence efficiency44. We envisage using recently developed MOF material processing to finely control the crystal orientation and position, and thus tune the exciton transport properties47–49. Fast triplet exciton diffusion in suitably designed MOFs would offer a new avenue towards MOF-based optoelectronics that would be applicable in many research areas, including photovoltaics, photocatalysts and light-emitting diodes.
