## Methods
Materials. All reagents and solvents were used as received unless otherwise noted. \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) octaethylporphyrin (PtOEP) and poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA, \(M_{\mathrm{w}}\sim120{,}000)\) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. Pd(II) mesoporphyrin IX (PdMesoIX) was purchased from Frontier Scientific. For spectroscopic measurements, we used spectral grade benzene purchased from TCI.
Synthesis. The ligand \(4,4^{\prime}\) -(anthracene-9,10-diyl)dibenzoic acid (adba) was synthesized following the literature procedures28. The MOF crystals for single-crystal X-ray analysis were synthesized by the solvothermal method. For liquid dispersion samples, we synthesized MOF particles by using the microwave method instead of the solvothermal method because it gives better particle dispersibility in benzene, probably owing to the smaller crystal size. Microwave reactions were performed using a Biotage Initiator 2.5 under continuous stirring. The power was approximately \(31{-}33\,\mathrm{W}\) with a temperature rise \(3{-}4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . No pressure increase was observed during the synthesis. For the synthesis of MOF 1, 3 mixture of adba \((20\,\mathrm{mg},0.048\,\mathrm{mmol})\) , \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ( \(14\,\mathrm{mg},0.048\,\mathrm{mmol})\) , \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -bipyridine (bpy) \((3.75\,\mathrm{mg},0.024\,\mathrm{mmol})\) and \(2.5\,\mathrm{ml}\,\mathrm{N},N^{\prime}\) -dimethylformamide (DMF) was placed in a sealed glass vial and microwave-heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) , filtered through a \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) filter to remove small crystals, washed several times with DMF, and subsequently dried under vacuum at room temperature. Yield \(=20.5\,\mathrm{mg}\) \((35\%)\) . Elemental analysis for \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{16}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{N}_{2})]\) :DMF \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) : calcd. \((\%)\) : C 68.44, H 4.08, N 3.47; found \((\%)\) : C 67.94, H 4.08, N 3.52. For the synthesis of MOF 2, (ref. 28) the reaction conditions are similar to the microwave synthesis of MOF 1, but changing the co-ligand from bpy to 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane (dabco). Yield \(=18.2\,\mathrm{mg}\,(32.3\%)\) . Elemental analysis for \([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{16}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{N}_{2})]\) :DMF \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) : calcd. \((\%)\) : C 66.90, H 4.58, N 3.6; found \((\%)\) : C 66.53, H 4.47, N 3.62. For the synthesis of MOF 3, a mixture of adba \((20\,\mathrm{mg},0.048\,\mathrm{mmol})\) , \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) ( \(14\,\mathrm{mg},0.048\,\mathrm{mmol})\) and \(2.5\,\mathrm{ml}\) \(N,N^{\prime}\) -diethylformamide (DEF) was placed in a sealed glass vial, microwave-heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) , filtered through a \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) filter to remove small crystals, washed several times with DEF, and subsequently dried under vacuum at room temperature. Yield \(=11\,\mathrm{mg}\,(33\%)\) . Elemental analysis for \([\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{16}\mathrm{O}_{4})(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{11}\mathrm{NO})_{2}]\) : calcd. \((\%)\) : C 66.72, H 5.6, N 4.1; found \((\%)\) : C 66.23, H 5.55, N 4.15. For single-crystal X-ray structure analysis, crystals were synthesized by the solvothermal method using a Teflon autoclave and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) , then cooled to room temperature at a rate of \(1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) . The amounts of reagent and solvent are identical to the measurements.
Nanosized MOF 3 was synthesized by rapid mixing of a \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) DEF solution of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{acetate})_{2}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((47.4\,\mathrm{mM})\) and a \(3\,\mathrm{ml}\) DEF solution of adba \((1.59\,\mathrm{mM})\) at room temperature with continuous stirring \((1,300\,\mathrm{r.p.m.})\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\) , quickly centrifuging and washing several times with DEF, followed by drying in vacuum at room temperature. Yield \(=0.3\,\mathrm{mg}\,(10\%)\) . This synthesis was repeated to obtain enough material to proceed to the next step. The surface modification of the nanocrystals with donor Pd(II) mesoporphyrin IX (PdMesoIX) was done by incubating the crystals \((3\,\mathrm{mg})\) in a DEF solution of PdMesoIX \((1.4\,\mathrm{mM},\,120\,\upmu\mathrm{l})\) at room temperature for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) , washing several times with DEF by centrifugation, and drying under vacuum at room temperature.
Sample preparation for TTA-UC emission measurements. The dried MOF crystals were dispersed in benzene solution of PtOEP \(\left(\mathrm{PtOEP}\right)=18{-}30\,\upmu\mathrm{M},\) \([\mathrm{MOF}]=3\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}})\) . The dispersions were placed in standard freeze–pump–thaw cells that connect a small Shlenk flask with a \(1\,\mathrm{mm}\) quartz cell, and purged of oxygen by repeated freeze–pump–thaw cycles. The triplet diffusion constant was obtained by using equations (1) and (2) (ref. 35). The \(\alpha\) value was obtained by
The PdMesoIX-modified MOF 3 nanocrystals were dispersed in a toluene solution of PMMA \((40\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) , MOF/PMMA weight ratio is 1:10) in an Ar-filled glove box, and this dispersion was cast on a quartz slide, dried under vacuum, and then sealed in the Ar atmosphere.
Measurements. Elemental analysis was conducted at the Elemental Analysis Center, Kyushu University. X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD) analysis was conducted on a Rigaku SmartLab with a copper Kα source. Single-crystal X-ray data were collected on a CCD diffractometer (Rigaku Saturn VariMax) with graphite-monochromated Mo Kα radiation \((M_{\mathrm{E}}\!=\!0.71070\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) pictures were taken on a Hitachi S-5000 (acceleration voltage, \(10\,\mathrm{kV})\) . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was conducted on a JEOL JEM-2010 (acceleration voltage, \(120\,\mathrm{kV})\) . Ultraviolet–visible absorption spectra were recorded on a JASCO V-670 spectrophotometer at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . A quartz cell with a \(1\,\mathrm{mm}\) path length was used. Emission spectra were recorded on a PerkinElmer LS55 spectrophotometer with a \(1\,\mathrm{mm}\) path length cell. The samples were excited at an incidence angle of \(45^{\circ}\) to the quartz cell surface and the fluorescence was detected along the normal. The absolute quantum yields were measured using a Hamamatsu C9920-02G set-up with an integrating sphere. Time-resolved fluorescence lifetime measurements were carried out using a time-correlated single-photon counting lifetime spectroscopy system, HAMAMATSU (Quantaurus-Tau C11367-02 (for fluorescence lifetime)/C11567-01 (for delayed luminescence lifetime). The upconversion luminescence emission spectra were recorded on an Otsuka Electronics MCPD-7000 instrument with the excitation source using an external, adjustable \(532\,\mathrm{nm}\) semiconductor laser.
